  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct evidence – CFTR loss → dysbiosis**  \n- Mice with intestinal‑epithelial‑specific Cftr knockout develop marked shifts in microbial composition (↑Proteobacteria, Clostridia, Akkermansia changes) without any other genetic alteration, demonstrating that loss of epithelial CFTR alone is sufficient to drive dysbiosis【PMID:37546931】.  \n- Bone‑marrow‑transplant studies show that deleting cftr in epithelial cells (large‑intestine) and in immune cells (small‑intestine) each produces distinct microbial disturbances, confirming that CFTR activity in host cells is a primary determinant of gut community structure【PMID:36289287】.  \n- Whole‑body CFTR‑KO mice exhibit a dysbiotic signature (↑Enterobacteriaceae, Enterococcus) that disappears when CFTR is restored only in the gut (CFTR‑KO‑GC), indicating that intestinal CFTR is necessary for maintaining the pathogenic microbiota【PMID:38554847】.  \n\n**Direct evidence – dysbiosis → inflammation**  \n- In the same iCftr‑KO mice, fecal calprotectin (a marker of intestinal inflammation) is significantly elevated and correlates with the observed dysbiotic patterns (Proteobacteria and mucolytic taxa expansion)【PMID:37546931】.  \n- CFTR‑KO mice show increased intestinal permeability together with mucosal inflammation; non‑absorbable antibiotics that blunt the dysbiotic overgrowth also reduce permeability and liver inflammation, establishing a causal link from the altered microbiota to inflammatory pathology【PMID:38554847】.  \n- In humans, ivacaftor‑mediated CFTR potentiation increases the anti‑inflammatory genus Akkermansia and reduces Enterobacteriaceae; these compositional changes accompany a significant fall in stool calprotectin, linking microbiota normalization to reduced gut inflammation【PMID:30546102】.  \n\n**Associative evidence (CFTR ↔ inflammation without explicit dysbiosis measurement)**  \n- Review of CF mouse models notes that CFTR dysfunction leads to mucus stasis, bacterial overgrowth and innate immune activation, producing intestinal inflammation that is likely mediated by microbial imbalance, though direct causality was not isolated【PMID:24548777】.  \n\n**Testable hypothesis**  \n- Restoring CFTR function solely in intestinal epithelial cells will normalize the gut microbiome and consequently lower both local (calprotectin)"
  },
